---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ComponentDocs from "../../../components/ComponentDocs.astro";
---

<Layout title="HTML text">
  <ComponentDocs title="HTML text" native>
    <p slot="description">
      The flowing-text primitives — headings, paragraphs and inline marks, links, inline
      code, blockquotes, lists, rules — as <code>elements.css</code> grounds them, plus the
      <code>.hp-prose</code> rhythm scope for long-form content. Write plain HTML; the
      system's typography, colour, and focus discipline apply on their own.
    </p>

    <h2>Headings</h2>
    <hp-demo caption="h1 through h6">
      <div>
        <h1>Heading 1 — display lg</h1>
        <h2>Heading 2 — headline lg</h2>
        <h3>Heading 3 — headline md</h3>
        <h4>Heading 4 — title md</h4>
        <h5>Heading 5 — label md (uppercase)</h5>
        <h6>Heading 6 — label sm (uppercase)</h6>
      </div>
      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <h1>Heading 1 — display lg</h1>
  <h2>Heading 2 — headline lg</h2>
  <h3>Heading 3 — headline md</h3>
  <h4>Heading 4 — title md</h4>
  <h5>Heading 5 — label md (uppercase)</h5>
  <h6>Heading 6 — label sm (uppercase)</h6>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>
    <p>
      Headings carry zero default margin so they compose freely. Use <code>.hp-prose</code>
      on a wrapping element to opt into vertical rhythm.
    </p>

    <h2>Inline text</h2>
    <hp-demo caption="Emphasis, marks, abbreviations">
      <div>
        <p>
          Paragraphs default to <code>--hp-on-surface</code>. <strong>Strong</strong> and
          <b>b</b> tighten weight; <em>emphasis</em> and <i>i</i> italicise.
          <small>Small text drops to body-sm.</small>
        </p>
        <p>
          <mark>Marked text</mark> uses the warn container tokens.
          <del>Deleted</del> strikes through; <ins>inserted</ins> underlines with the
          secondary hue. Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>K</kbd> for command palettes —
          <abbr title="Keyboard">kbd</abbr> reads as a key cap.
        </p>
        <p>
          H<sub>2</sub>O and E = mc<sup>2</sup>. Variables read as <var>x</var>, samples as
          <samp>OK</samp>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <p>
    <strong>Strong</strong> / <b>b</b>, <em>emphasis</em> / <i>i</i>,
    <small>small text</small>, <mark>marked</mark>, <del>del</del>,
    <ins>ins</ins>, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>, <abbr title="Keyboard">kbd</abbr>.
  </p>
  <p>H<sub>2</sub>O — E = mc<sup>2</sup>. <var>x</var> / <samp>OK</samp>.</p>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>

    <h2>Links</h2>
    <hp-demo caption="Anchor as text">
      <p>
        Raw <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> is the *anchor-as-text* primitive — for in-sentence
        references like a <a href="#">link to elsewhere</a>. For *anchor-as-affordance*
        (hover trace, hex geometry), reach for
        <a href="/components/typography/hp-link"><code>&lt;hp-link&gt;</code></a> or
        <a href="/components/hex-core/hp-cell"><code>&lt;hp-cell variant="anchor"&gt;</code></a>.
      </p>
      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <p>
    Visit <a href="https://example.com">example.com</a> for details.
  </p>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>

    <h2>Code</h2>
    <hp-banner tone="neutral">
      For syntax-highlighted code with line numbers, per-line hover, and a copy button,
      reach for <a href="/components/typography/hp-code"><code>&lt;hp-code&gt;</code></a> —
      it accepts a consumer-registered highlighter (Shiki, highlight.js, Prism). The raw
      primitives below are the right call for short inline snippets and unhighlighted
      block samples.
    </hp-banner>
    <hp-demo caption="Inline and block">
      <div>
        <p>
          Inline <code>const x = 1</code> renders as a chip. Block-level code uses
          <code>&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;</code>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>function hex(q, r) &#123;
  return [q * (3 / 2), r * Math.sqrt(3) + q * Math.sqrt(3) / 2];
&#125;</code></pre>
      </div>
      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <p>Inline <code>const x = 1</code> reads as a chip.</p>
  <pre>
    <code>
      function hex(q, r) {
        return [q * (3 / 2), r * Math.sqrt(3) + q * Math.sqrt(3) / 2];
      }
    </code>
  </pre>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>

    <h2>Blockquote</h2>
    <hp-demo caption="Citation with attribution">
      <blockquote>
        <p>The system is the source of truth — the chrome is just the surface.</p>
        <cite>— Hexpunk DESIGN.md</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <blockquote>
    <p>The system is the source of truth — the chrome is just the surface.</p>
    <cite>— Hexpunk DESIGN.md</cite>
  </blockquote>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>

    <h2>Lists</h2>
    <hp-demo caption="Unordered, ordered, and definition lists">
      <div class="hp-prose">
        <ul>
          <li>Atoms — single-hex elements (<code>hp-cell</code>, <code>hp-hex</code>)</li>
          <li>Molecules — composed structures (<code>hp-grid</code>, <code>hp-layout</code>)</li>
          <li>
            Primitives — the bigger ideas
            <ul>
              <li>Bonding (adjacency)</li>
              <li>Linking (arcs at distance)</li>
              <li>Unfolding (in-place / overlay / camera-zoom)</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <ol>
          <li>Read DESIGN.md</li>
          <li>Read PLAN.md</li>
          <li>Pick a step, propose, confirm, ship</li>
        </ol>
        <dl>
          <dt>Cell</dt>
          <dd>Universal labelled hex element with interactive variants.</dd>
          <dt>Tether</dt>
          <dd>Curved arc between two anchors.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <div class="hp-prose">
    <ul>
      <li>Atoms — single-hex elements</li>
      <li>
        Molecules — composed structures
        <ul>
          <li>Bonding (adjacency)</li>
          <li>Linking (arcs at distance)</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <ol>
      <li>Read DESIGN.md</li>
      <li>Pick a step, propose, confirm, ship</li>
    </ol>
    <dl>
      <dt>Cell</dt>
      <dd>Universal labelled hex element.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>
    <p>
      Bullet glyphs use the secondary hue via <code>::marker</code>. Padding shown here comes
      from the <code>.hp-prose</code> wrapper — bare-reset lists ship with zero padding so
      consumers control list indentation per-surface.
    </p>

    <h2>Horizontal rule</h2>
    <hp-banner tone="neutral">
      For dividers with orientation (horizontal / vertical), tone, and optional centred
      marks, use <a href="/components/layout/hp-separator"><code>&lt;hp-separator&gt;</code></a>.
      Raw <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code> stays useful for inline section breaks inside long-form
      content.
    </hp-banner>
    <hp-demo caption="Section divider">
      <div>
        <p>Above the rule.</p>
        <hr>
        <p>Below the rule.</p>
      </div>
      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <p>Above the rule.</p>
  <hr />
  <p>Below the rule.</p>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>

    <h2 id="visually-hidden">Visually hidden</h2>
    <hp-demo caption="Icon control with a hidden label">
      <hp-button size="sm">
        <span aria-hidden="true">🔍</span>
        <span class="hp-visually-hidden">search</span>
      </hp-button>
      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <hp-button>
    <svg aria-hidden="true">…</svg>
    <span class="hp-visually-hidden">Search</span>
  </hp-button>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>
    <p>
      <code>.hp-visually-hidden</code> keeps content out of view but readable by assistive
      tech — icon-only controls that need an accessible name, live announcements, skip
      links. It works anywhere in light DOM, including content slotted into
      <code>hp-*</code> elements.
    </p>

    <h2>.hp-prose — long-form rhythm</h2>
    <p>
      The bare reset ships zero vertical rhythm — no heading margins, no paragraph spacing,
      no list padding. Drop <code>class="hp-prose"</code> on a container to opt in for
      long-form content. Same HTML below, with and without the wrapper.
    </p>
    <hp-demo caption="Without .hp-prose">
      <div style="border: 1px dashed var(--hp-outline-faint); padding: 16px;">
        <h3>The hex stencil</h3>
        <p>
          Every hex has a bounding box of <code>w × (w · 2/√3)</code>. Axial
          coordinates project to pixels as <code>x = w · (q + r/2)</code>,
          <code>y = w · √3/2 · r</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The grid uses an effective cell width of <code>w − hex-stroke</code> so
          adjacent hexes' stroke bands coincide into a single shared edge.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Pointy-top is the default orientation.</li>
          <li>Flat-top kicks in at <code>size="md"</code>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <div>
    <h3>The hex stencil</h3>
    <p>Every hex has a bounding box of <code>w × (w · 2/√3)</code>...</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Pointy-top is the default orientation.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>
    <hp-demo caption="With .hp-prose">
      <div class="hp-prose" style="border: 1px dashed var(--hp-outline-faint); padding: 16px;">
        <h3>The hex stencil</h3>
        <p>
          Every hex has a bounding box of <code>w × (w · 2/√3)</code>. Axial
          coordinates project to pixels as <code>x = w · (q + r/2)</code>,
          <code>y = w · √3/2 · r</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The grid uses an effective cell width of <code>w − hex-stroke</code> so
          adjacent hexes' stroke bands coincide into a single shared edge.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Pointy-top is the default orientation.</li>
          <li>Flat-top kicks in at <code>size="md"</code>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <div class="hp-prose">
    <h3>The hex stencil</h3>
    <p>Every hex has a bounding box of <code>w × (w · 2/√3)</code>...</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Pointy-top is the default orientation.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>

    <hp-demo caption="Successive headings tighten">
      <article class="hp-prose">
        <h2>The Foundation</h2>
        <h3>Subsection right under</h3>
        <p>
          Two headings stacked back-to-back don't get the full
          <code>--hp-xl</code> gap — they tighten so a subtitle hugs its parent. Below the
          second heading, paragraphs resume normal rhythm.
        </p>
        <p>
          A follow-up paragraph spaces normally — <code>--hp-md</code> from the previous
          block.
        </p>
        <h3>The next subsection</h3>
        <p>And a new section starts again.</p>
      </article>
      <hp-code slot="code" language="html">{`<body>
  <article class="hp-prose">
    <h2>The Foundation</h2>
    <h3>Subsection right under</h3>
    <p>Two headings stacked back-to-back tighten...</p>
    <p>A follow-up paragraph spaces normally...</p>
    <h3>The next subsection</h3>
    <p>And a new section starts again.</p>
  </article>
</body>`}</hp-code>
    </hp-demo>
    <p>
      The rhythm rules: sibling blocks space by <code>--hp-md</code>; headings get
      <code>--hp-xl</code> above and <code>--hp-sm</code> below (tightening when stacked,
      resetting at the top of the wrapper); lists indent by <code>--hp-lg</code> with tinted
      markers; blockquote / table / figure / pre match the block cadence; line length caps
      at <code>70ch</code> — override on the wrapper if you need wider.
    </p>

    <Fragment slot="intent">
      <h2>When to reach for what</h2>
      <hp-banner tone="do">
        Write plain HTML for text. Wrap the content surface — not the page shell — in
        <code>.hp-prose</code>: a typical layout sets
        <code>&lt;article class="hp-prose"&gt;</code> around the rendered body and leaves
        nav / sidebar / footer outside.
      </hp-banner>
      <hp-banner tone="do">
        Use <code>.hp-prose</code> for article bodies, README renders, MDX output, docs
        prose, changelog summaries — anywhere a block of HTML wants to read like a
        document.
      </hp-banner>
      <hp-banner tone="dont">
        Put <code>.hp-prose</code> on app chrome — settings panes, dashboards, form
        layouts are bespoke per-screen and don't want forced <code>70ch</code> caps or
        sibling margins. Component internals are shadow DOM; the class can't and shouldn't
        reach into them.
      </hp-banner>
      <hp-banner tone="dont">
        Recreate text primitives as components. <code>&lt;hp-link&gt;</code> exists for
        anchor-as-affordance and <code>&lt;hp-code&gt;</code> for highlighted blocks —
        everything else here is deliberately just HTML.
      </hp-banner>
    </Fragment>
  </ComponentDocs>
</Layout>
